  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t+9{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =3(1)^{2}+3(1)^{3}+C \\\\ 6 & =6+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(1)^{3}+\\frac{3}{4}(1)^{4}+K \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{7}{4}+K \\\\ K & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$",
